{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And he made it a molten calf.</b> The Ramban wrote: that they made the form of a calf — Aaron was the one who produced this form, for those who asked said only in general terms, 'Arise, make us elohim,' without disclosing their intent to tell him whether to make an ox or a sheep or a goat. And Aaron's intention was because Israel was in the wilderness of Horeb, a desolation, and destruction and desolation always come from the north, as is said (Jeremiah 1:14): 'מצפון תפתח הרעה על כל יושבי הארץ' — the intent is not the king of Babylon alone as appears on the surface of the verse, but rather that the attribute of judgment comes from the left side always, to requite all the inhabitants of the earth according to their evil. Now, in the work of the Chariot, 'ופני שור מהשמאל לארבעתם' — the face of an ox is on the left for all four of them — and therefore Aaron reasoned that the destroyer points to destruction, for there its great power lies; and when they would worship there, a spirit would flow from on high just as it was emanated upon Moses. And this is what he said, 'חג לה׳ מחר' — a festival to YHWH tomorrow — that the worship and the sacrifices would be for the Name, the distinctive one, to draw favor from Him to the master of the form, for when it is before them they would direct their intent to its matter. And our Sages of blessed memory taught us this matter, they who revealed its secret, saying (Exodus 3:7): 'ראה ראיתי את עני עמי' — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: Moses, you see one vision and I see two; you see them coming to Sinai and receiving My Torah, and I see them how they contemplate Me when I go forth in My chariots to give them the Torah, as is said (Psalms 68:18): 'רכב אלהים רבותים' etc., and they pluck one of My tetramulin, as is written (Ezekiel 1:10): 'ופני שור מהשמאל לארבעתם,' and provoke Me with it. Tetra means four; mulin means mules, like the mula'ot of the house of the master; and tetramulin is a metaphor for the four living creatures that bear the Chariot — end of quote." "<b>And the sage</b> R. E. of blessed memory said that 'elohim' in this place where they said, 'Arise, make us elohim,' is a glory that alights in the form of a body, and if you set your mind to the first journey you will understand this — end of quote. And the first journey is what is written: 'ויסע מלאך האלהים' etc. And with the language he used — 'a glory that alights in the form of a body' — the Ramban seized upon him and said: it is not correct in my eyes, that through the craft of the wisdom of the constellations there should alight in its form a glory or speech — end of quote. And the truth is that the making of the calf was not like the making of images, to receive the spirituality of a star or constellation and draw it down there, for the intent was above the stars and constellations. And this is what Aaron intended in making the calf: a body capable of receiving one force from the tetramulin of the Holy One, blessed be He, which is the ox in the Chariot, which is on the left, the attribute of judgment, in order that it might show them the way, for the destroyer indicates the way of destruction, and Israel was in the wilderness, in a place of destruction, which is the domain of the star Mars, and all its forces are poured forth and draw from the attribute of judgment. This was Aaron's intention in making the calf — that their journey would be through it from the side of the attribute of judgment, just as it had been through Moses from the side of the attribute of judgment, as is written (Numbers 9:23): 'על פי ה׳ ביד משה' — at the mouth of HaShem by the hand of Moses. And after he finished making it, he built an altar before it, and in this there was a cutting of the shoots (i.e., an act of separation), except that his intention in the sacrifices was for the distinctive Name — this is what he said, 'חג לה׳ מחר' — a festival to YHWH tomorrow — and he did not say 'to elohim' or 'to the calf'; for had his intent been for the attribute of judgment alone, there would have been in this a cutting (of shoots)." "<b>And you should know</b> that what they said, 'אלה אלהיך ישראל' — had they said this with good intent, there would have been no idolatry in it, for in truth the attribute of judgment did assist them on that ascent, as is written (Exodus 14:19): 'ויסע מלאך האלהים' etc. But the sin was in what is written: 'וישתחוו לו ויזבחו לו' — they bowed to it and they sacrificed to it — for Aaron's intention regarding the calf was for the attribute of judgment and not to remove them from the domain of the distinctive Name; but Israel reversed this in the very body of the calf when they worshipped it. And this is what is written (Psalms 106:20): 'וימירו את כבודם בתבנית שור' etc. And this is the language of Aaron: 'ואמר להם למי זהב התפרקו' etc. 'ואשליכהו באש' — he says: I said to them, 'to whom is the gold?' and I cast it into the fire — for my intent was for the attribute of judgment, and this calf came forth that you see before you, for toward him they were directing their intent, bowing and sacrificing. And behold, it is the opposite of my intention, which was that their journey would be from the side of the attribute of judgment and that the intent would be for the distinctive Name alone, and we would bring to Him the burnt-offering and the sacrifices; but they sinned in it and cut off (the shoots). And since Aaron produced this form with the intention that their journey would be from the side of that attribute, not to make a likeness of it, heaven forfend, you have no grounds to ask why he chose gold and said, 'פרקו נזמי הזהב,' and did not choose silver — since you already know that gold belongs to the attribute of judgment, and its appearance is like the appearance of fire. And our Sages of blessed memory interpreted (II Chronicles 3:6): 'זהב פרוים' — gold of Parvaim — that it resembles the blood of oxen. And the House of Sacrifices was entirely of gold, and likewise the altar of incense and the cherubim, and they interpreted regarding the cherubim that if one made them of silver they would be like 'elohei kesef' and 'elohei zahav' — silver elohim and gold elohim." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'ויעשהו עגל מסכה' — once he cast the gold into the furnace, the sorcerers of the mixed multitude that came up with them from Egypt arrived and made it by sorcery. And some say Micah was there, who emerged from the mortar of the building that was crushed in Egypt, and in his hand was a name through which Moses had written 'עלה שור עלה שור' — 'go up, ox; go up, ox' — in order to bring up the ark of Joseph from the Nile, and he cast it into the furnace, and the calf came forth lowing, while it was prosecuting. They began to say, 'אלה אלהיך ישראל'; therefore he was called Micah, after the name of the building that was crushed (נתמעך)." "<b>And I saw</b> in the chapters of Rabbi Eliezer: Aaron found among the ornaments one golden plate upon which the holy Name was written, and engraved upon it was the form of a calf, and that one alone he cast into the furnace of fire, as is said: 'ויתנו לי ואשליכם בכור' — it is not written here, but rather 'ואשליכהו באש' — and I cast it (singular) into the fire — and the calf came forth lowing, and all Israel saw it and went astray after it. Rabbi Yudah says: Samael entered into it and it lowed, to lead Israel astray, as is said (Isaiah 1:3): 'ידע שור קונהו' — end of quote, in chapter 45 of the chapters." "<b>These are your Elohim, O Israel.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: this teaches that they desired a plurality of divinities. They derived this from the language of אלה, which is in the plural; for were it not so, it should have said זה אלהיך ישראל — 'this is your Elohim, O Israel' — in the same language that Ezra said (Nehemiah 9:18): ״אף כי עשו להם עגל מסכה ויאמרו זה אלהיך אשר העלך ממצרים״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "Rabbeinu Bahya quotes the Ramban; the closing parenthetical citation לארבעתם is part of the Ezekiel quotation, not a separate reference. ע\"כ (עד כדי) marks the end of the quoted midrash.", 2 "R. E. in the lemma refers to R. Eli'ezer, matching the ר\"א in the preceding comment. The citation II Chronicles 3:6 is explicit in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:42.380671629Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}